  5. Smart Chunking Strategy for Logs
  
  Where in code: LogIngestionService.chunkLogEntries() and splitIntoLogEntries()
  
  This is one of the most important concepts in RAG. Chunking determines what the AI can find. Bad chunking = bad retrieval = bad answers.
  
  Your code implements a two-phase chunking strategy:
  
  Phase A — Split into logical log entries:
  
  private List<String> splitIntoLogEntries(String logContent) {
      // A new entry starts when a line begins with a timestamp
      // Lines WITHOUT a timestamp (stack traces) stay with the previous entry
  }
  
  This is critical: if you naively split by newline, a stack trace gets separated from its ERROR line, and the AI loses context.
  
  Example — this stays as ONE entry:
  
  2026-04-25 10:00:01 ERROR db Connection failed
  java.sql.SQLException: Connection refused
      at com.db.Pool.getConnection(Pool.java:42)
      at com.app.Service.query(Service.java:15)
  
  Phase B — Group entries into chunks up to 1000 chars:
  
  List<String> chunkLogEntries(String logContent) {
      // Group small entries together until MAX_CHUNK_CHARS is reached
      // Start a new chunk when adding the next entry would exceed the limit
  }
  
  Why group? Because embedding a single short log line like "INFO app Started" wastes a vector. Grouping related entries into a chunk gives the embedding
  model more context to work with.
  
  The trade-off: Smaller chunks = more precise retrieval but less context per chunk. Larger chunks = more context but may include irrelevant lines. The
  1000-char limit is a reasonable middle ground.
  
  Learn more:
  
  - https://docs.langchain4j.dev/tutorials/rag#document-splitter (https://docs.langchain4j.dev/tutorials/rag#document-splitter) — LangChain4j's built-in
  splitters
  - https://www.pinecone.io/learn/chunking-strategies/ (https://www.pinecone.io/learn/chunking-strategies/) — Excellent guide on chunking strategies
  
